73), and 4 -> 2 came with many other changes (F79). Neither shows that lower is better, and selling under cost lowers Book at once.", "when": ["11/10 07:19"]},\n{"rule": "market_ask is not a proven lever: 0.9 -> 0.95 made no difference beyond noise (267/5711 -> 242/5675 sales). Do not spend cycles retuning it; put them on levers with a measured effect.", "evidence": ["F76"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Reading raw margin moves after market_ask changes (F103 +102%, F109 -56%, F121 +63%) as market_ask effects. Each came with other changes in the same hours, so they are MIXED and no evidence.", "when": ["11/11 03:09"]},\n{"rule": "Only four shop numbers have a one-at-a-time verdict: floor_frac_food (F70), floor_frac (F71), stuck_hours (F72) and market_ask (F76). Only these may support a lesson. A MIXED change, one that shares its hours with another, is no evidence whatever its raw margin says. In play mode, move different numbers in the same cycle only if you can say which one the evidence supports, and check the value in force before each move (orders 5 and 9).", "evidence": ["F70", "F71", "F72", "F76"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Writing up raw margin swings as wins or losses, such as +497% (F74) and +125% (F101), or acting on audit data measured at an old value. On 11/12 ask_frac stepped 0.8 -> 0.85 -> 0.8 (F113, F114, F115) and the food floor 0.5 -> 0.52 (F111, F112) on such data, and the supervisor reverted both.", "when": ["11/10 01:43", "11/10 05:04", "11/10 07:19", "11/11 03:09"]}\n]}'
