"rule": "FIRM_GAP: leave it at 0.07 (read the value now first). Both directions lost on points/h: 0.07 -> 0.05 gave -35% at -1.1 sd (11/09 23:48) and -18% at -0.5 sd (11/11 11:18); 0.07 -> 0.09 gave -42% at -2.6 sd (11/12 11:03) and only +2% at -0.2 sd (11/09 18:04). A change judged in both directions is not repeated.", "evidence": ["F50", "F52", "F61", "F68"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Older rows kept 0.05 and 0.07 on saved/deal windows (11/06 14:50, 11/06 20:57, 11/07 10:29, 11/08 17:55) that flipped with the hour. Citing them restarts a loop that points/h has already closed.", "when": ["11/09 18:04", "11/09 23:48", "11/11 11:18", "11/12 11:03"]},\n{"rule": "CAP: read the value now first. Neither neighbour clearly beat 0.75 on points/h: 0.75 -> 0.8 gave -5% (11/10 03:43); 0.75 -> 0.7 gave +2% at +0.5 sd (11/10 12:26) and +0% at +0.5 sd (11/12 05:22). The differences are noise, so spend no more trials here and do not use CAP as the lever for points.", "evidence": ["F53", "F56", "F67"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Older CAP verdicts (11/06 to 11/08) were saved/deal windows that flipped with the hour, and the supervisor\'s 0.7 try and its undo (11/09 15:57, 11/09 16:14) carry no verdict. Citing them as proof for 0.8, 0.75 or 0.7 is wrong. Saved/deal rose slightly at 0.7 while points/h stayed flat.", "when": ["11/10 03:43", "11/10 12:26", "11/12 05:22"]},\n{"rule": "Keep the raise limits where they are (read the values now first: MAX_RAISE 0.35, RAISE_GOODS 0.8, RAISE_FOOD 1.2). MAX_RAISE 0.4 -> 0.35 won +3% (11/09 10:35), but going lower lost: 0.35 -> 0.3 gave -20% on 11/11 14:49 and +1% on 11/10 08:39; 0.35 -> 0.25 gave -2%. Raising RAISE_GOODS 0.8 -> 0.9 gave -3% and RAISE_FOOD 1.2 -> 1.3 gave -14%.", "evidence": ["F46", "F54", "F62", "F63", "F65", "F69"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Close rate rose in some windows (RAISE_GOODS 0.695 -> 0.729) and saved/deal rose in others (MAX_RAISE 0.4053 -> 0.4097) while points/h fell. Reading those parts as a win would loosen a limit that points/h says to keep.", "when": ["11/09 10:35", "11/10 08:39", "11/11 14:49", "11/11 19:08", "11/12 01:27", "11/12 14:51"]},\n{"rule": "Be doubtful of new ranking, scouting or penalty code that promises a gain: most of it measured at or below zero on its own metric. Reverted: scout_wave expected-saved-rate ranking (11/11 00:28, -5%), over-cap exponent 8 (11/12 05:07, -9% close rate). Also negative: break-rate weighting (-6%), grade-penalty ranking (-4%), soft-breaks ban (-2%). The accept_policy change was flat (+0%). Only the short-shelf-life penalty cut (+3% close rate) and the scouted over-cap drop (+22% seconds per order) measured positive, each on a part. Ship such code only behind an A/B flag and judge it on points/h.", "evidence": ["F39", "F42", "F55", "F58", "F59", "F66", "F70", "F72"], "status": "verified", "trap": "The meta-agent\'s expected gains were +1% to +4% in every row, yet most measured negative, and the positive ones were only on parts (close rate, seconds per order). Taking an expected gain, or a part, as proof of more board points repeats the mistake.", "when": ["11/08 16:36", "11/08 21:36", "11/10 09:28", "11/10 17:41", "11/11 00:28", "11/12 05:07", "11/12 16:48", "11/12 21:47"]},\n{"rule": "Code meant to shorten talks (early cancel, earlier walk-away) must show that it shortens them before it stays, and must also be checked on points/h. Two tries missed their own target; the scouting drop of over-cap shops did shorten orders (expected +10%, measured +22%) but its effect on points/h is not in the record.", "evidence": ["F16", "F49", "F72"], "status": "verified", "trap": "11/05 17:22 expected -8% seconds per order and measured +1%; 11/09 16:37 expected -5% and measured +13%. Both were marked kept, so the label hid that the change did not do what it claimed. A speed gain alone does not prove a points gain.", "when": ["11/05 17:22", "11/09 16:37", "11/12 21:47"]},\n{"rule": "MAX_TRIES 6 -> 5 has one points/h read: +14% at +0