at capital does for points."}, \n{"rule": "Do not recommend values from the 11/11 11:44-13:33 sweep, which changed up to six numbers per cycle (goods_frac, food_first, clear_age_hours, min_margin, dear_depth, surge_max). Cycle 11/11 13:42 reversed much of it, and it carries no verdict.", "evidence": ["F109", "F113", "F114", "F117", "F118"], "status": "contradicted", "trap": "Citing the raw margin gains beside the sweep rows (+53%, +49%, +32%) as support for those values. Every row carries the same other changes, so none can be credited to a single number."}, \n{"rule": "Three stale or trial-unit goods guards in restock_r3.py were kept but measured below expectation on shop revenue (F123 -37%, F158 -6%, F164 -17%), and the A-only veto change was reverted by its guard (F143). All of these rows are MIXED, so this is not proof that the guards hurt. Do not stack another guard of this kind without reading its effect on turns first, and do not re-ship the reverted veto change.", "evidence": ["F123", "F143", "F158", "F164"], "status": "unverified", "trap": "Keeping a rule because its test file passes or its expected gain was positive. The measured revenue went the other way in three of the four rows, and F143 was reverted."}, \n{"rule": "Do not flip a number back within minutes or hours. window_depth went 2.5 -> 2.8 and 2.8 -> 2.5 in the same minute (F174, F175). fresh_premium and dear_depth were changed and undone one minute later (F182, F183), and the supervisor had to fix that (F184). Both pairs are MIXED and undo each other, so they give no information. Before moving a number, read its value now and check it has a closed 2 h verdict (orders 5 and 9).", "evidence": ["F174", "F175", "F182", "F183", "F184"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Taking a flip and its reversal as two tests. They net to nothing, yet they spend cycles and add confounds to the neighbouring rows."}\n]}'
