ints/h first and name the part second.", "evidence": ["F132", "F135", "F143", "F187"], "status": "unverified", "trap": "Calling a change a win because its target part (revenue or margin) rose while points/h fell, or reverting one because the part fell while points/h rose. These rows are also MIXED with number changes, so treat them as direction, not proof."},\n{"rule": "Restock throttles on slow lines (the one-trial-unit rule for goods SKUs, the stale-unit stop for not-hot goods SKUs under 150k, the stale no-heat rule) were each expected to raise revenue (+3%, +5%, +3%) and each measured lower on revenue and points. Do not add another throttle of this kind expecting turns to improve. First look for what freed capital there actually bought.", "evidence": ["F123", "F158", "F164"], "status": "contradicted", "trap": "Reading \'kept\' as \'worked\'. Revenue measured -37%, -6% and -17%, and points/h -167%, -64% and -57%, with the market\'s paid value moving x1.10, x1.28 and x0.94. All three are MIXED, so only the direction counts."},\n{"rule": "On hot 300k+ goods lines, keep the A-only margin veto strict and tie A depth to the share of A cost draws that can sell at or below the market median. Loosening the veto was reverted (revenue 12002597 -> 4428814.431343914). Valuing A by cost draws and charging A_HOLD storage was kept, margin +92%, points/h +335%. The depth scaling by sellable share (F195) has no verdict yet.", "evidence": ["F132", "F143", "F187", "F195"], "status": "unverified", "trap": "Skipping the veto on hot lines to buy deeper there. F143 shows it cut paid revenue by more than half. The kept rows are MIXED with number changes, so check the F195 verdict before building on it."}\n]}'
