[11/13 12:37:48] HARNESS: try 1: 26.2 s, exit 0, answer '{"lessons":[{"rule":"Number rows in play mode give no verdict: every number row from 11/12 09:00 to 11/13 10:04 is MIXED, and the raw margin figure beside one is not its effect. Choose the next number from the board gap and the rivals and edges tables. Keep several number changes in one cycle only if the evidence supports each, and do not credit any one of them with the move.","evidence":["F155","F172","F185","F190","F200","F201","F204","F206"],"status":"unverified","trap":"Reading raw margin swings as the effect of the one number moved: +222% at 11/12 04:16, +84% at 11/13 00:14, -70% at 11/12 01:16, -61% at 11/12 00:59, +136% at 11/13 09:20. Other changes fell in the same hours every time."},{"rule":"Do not add age-based or count-based throttles on slow goods lines (the stale-unit stop for not-hot goods SKUs under 150k, the stale no-heat rule) expecting more revenue. Both measured lower on revenue and on points/h. To free stuck capital, use the clearance rule (stuck rule, clear_floor) instead.","evidence":["F158","F164"],"status":"unverified","trap":"Reading \'kept\' as \'worked\'. F158 expected revenue +5% and measured -6%, with points/h -64%. F164 expected +3% and measured -17%, with points/h -57%. Both are confounded by number changes in the same hours, so the evidence is weak."},{"rule":"Gate buying on market price signals (surge factor, the market median for grade A cost draws), not on unit age. The two code changes of this kind measured large gains on margin and points/h. The depth scaling by sellable share did not.","evidence":["F132","F187","F195"],"status":"unverified","trap":"Extending the idea to every dear line. F195 scaled dear-line depth by the sellable A share and measured shop_margin_h -142% against +5% expected, with points/h -2%. F132 (points/h +810%) and F187 (+335%) were also MIXED with other changes, so size is not proof."},{"rule":"Judge a code change on board points/h, not on the single metric it was expected to move. The two disagree often, so read both before keeping or reverting, and when they split, points/h decides.","evidence":["F135","F143","F195"],"status":"unverified","trap":"F143 (the A-only veto skipping hot 300k+ lines) was reverted on shop_revenue_h -63% although points/h read +182%. F135 went the other way: revenue +23% but points/h -113%."},{"rule":"Do not repeat a move the supervisor already reverted without a new measurement taken after the revert. This covers market_ask lowerings and food-floor raises, which were each reverted. Standing orders 10 and 11 hold their values until their review times, and the supervisor\'s orders set the values, not the MIXED rows.","evidence":["F160","F178","F181","F186","F198"],"status":"unverified","trap":"Moving market_ask or the food floor again on a raw margin reading, or on \'the opposite direction is free\'. F160, F178 and F181 raised the food floor, F186 and F198 lowered market_ask, and all five rows are MIXED."},{"rule":"Do not flip a number back and forth within hours (dear_depth 4/5/6/7, window_depth 2.5/2.8, fresh_premium 0.15/0.18, max_list 600000/900000, sku_depth 2/3). Each flip overlaps the neighbouring changes and turns the window into MIXED rows. Move a number once, then wait for its closed 2 h verdict before reversing it.","evidence":["F174","F175","F182","F183","F184","F200","F201","F202","F204","F205","F206","F207"],"status":"unverified","trap":"Treating the opposite direction as free in play mode. F174 and F175 were opposite window_depth moves in the same minute, and F182 and F183 reversed each other one minute apart. F204 and F205 moved max_list in opposite directions at 11/13 09:20, and the supervisor reverted F184, F202 and F207."}]}'
