[11/13 14:16:58] HARNESS: try 1: 10.9 s, exit 0, answer '{"proposal": {}, "recruit": {"MAX_RAISE": 0.25, "FIRM_GAP": 0.05, "OPEN": "p25"}, "why": "Every buyer team currently has negative reward (-0.11 to -0.13) with OPEN low, and 436 of about 1,000 talks end in our walk with a median lost-talk gap of 0.328, so a higher opening offer is worth one trial. I leave the numbers alone because L2 and L4 forbid repeating FOOD_MARGIN or moving the raise limits without new evidence, L3 says CAP is noise, and the rest are at their bounds or lack evidence.", "ideas": ["tool: a per-grade buyer scorer that reports board points per hour for each buyer change, so the judgment rests on the board\'s buyer ALL rather than on our share of the market\'s paid sales.", "Cut the walk rate by changing what the buyer offers after a walk, not the numbers: the 0.328 lost-talk gap says our asks are far above what sellers accept, so a re-quote path for talks that end in walks may recover deals without a new raise limit."], "lessons_used": ["L1", "L2", "L3", "L4", "L5"]}'
[11/13 14:17:00] HARNESS: try 1: 12.3 s, exit 0, answer '{"proposal": {"sku_depth": 4}, "why": "We rank 20th with the gap in shop book and revenue, and stock at cost (69.9M) sits well under the 140M stock_value cap, so capacity is not binding. Our top demand SKUs (about 1,900 and 1,500 demand units/h) are stocked at depth 3, so one more unit per SKU adds sales where demand is highest and this does not reopen the reverted window_depth or dear-stock moves.", "ideas": ["tool: a per-SKU sell-through and age report (units bought, sold, and older than 24 h, by SKU and grade) so clearance of the 854 aged units can be targeted by SKU rather than by a global floor.", "No number can fix the buyer\'s deal quality (average deal near 0.67 of list on B/C goods), which is a buyer-side cost guess problem and not a shop setting."], "lessons_used": ["L1", "L4"]}'
