[11/14 14:44:24] HARNESS: try 1: 17.0 s, exit 0, answer '{"lessons":[{"rule":"Before stepping floor_frac_food, floor_frac, ask_frac or ask_frac_food, look for an earlier supervisor revert of that number in the record and in orders 9, 10 and 14. If one exists, step again only with a new measurement taken after the revert and cite its window.","evidence":["F112","F115","F117","F132","F141","F151","F156","F169"],"status":"unverified","trap":"The supervisor reverted floor_frac 0.68 -> 0.66 (F132, F141), floor_frac_food 0.57 -> 0.52 (F156) and ask_frac_food 0.83 -> 0.8 (F169). Each step came back on a thin \'different number\' or \'opposite direction\' argument and cost a cycle plus a revert. These are code rows with no verdict, so the lesson is about the process and is weak."},{"rule":"Never step a number a second time in the same direction before its last step has a closed 2 h verdict. Measure the next step at the value now in force, not at an older one.","evidence":["F106","F107","F113","F114","F115"],"status":"unverified","trap":"ask_frac went 0.72 -> 0.77, then 0.77 -> 0.8, then 0.8 -> 0.85 within about 3 hours with no verdict in between. The supervisor then reverted it to 0.8 (F114, F115). The same pattern would repeat with any number stepped on data measured at its old value."},{"rule":"Do not open a step and undo it in the same hour. Make one move per number per cycle and let it stand until a 2 h verdict closes.","evidence":["F130","F131","F139","F140","F149","F150","F154","F155","F177","F178","F182","F183"],"status":"unverified","trap":"floor_frac 0.66 -> 0.68 then 0.68 -> 0.66 landed in the same minute twice (F130/F131, F139/F140). fresh_premium 0.18 -> 0.15 and back at 11/14 10:13 (F177/F178), and clear_hours 10 -> 8 and back at 11/14 11:18 (F182/F183), repeated it. Each pair adds changes to the neighbouring hours, so the rows around it turn MIXED and teach nothing."},{"rule":"Do not cite a raw margin swing from a MIXED row as the effect of one number. Argue from board_pts_h, the rivals table and the edges table, and treat a swing only as a lead to measure.","evidence":["F109","F152","F165","F170","F175","F176"],"status":"unverified","trap":"Swings of -56% (F109), +83% (F152), +163% (F165), +290% (F170), +21% (F175) and +86% (F176) each came with other changes in the same hours. Reading any of them as one number\'s effect would back a wrong move and then a revert."},{"rule":"Before proposing a number, check order 14\'s list of values whose own verdict closed negative. A return to one needs a new measurement taken after it. A number moved away from a negative value is not a free \'different number\'.","evidence":["F157","F158","F171","F172","F177","F178"],"status":"unverified","trap":"fresh_premium was sent back to 0.15 three times (F157, F171, F177) and undone within a minute each time (F158, F172, F178), although order 14 lists 0.15 as a value whose own verdict closed negative. Each try cost a cycle and left MIXED rows around it."}]}'
