n the first three 1.1 trials (e.g. 0.3722 -> 0.3889) and the saved difference was positive each time, yet points/h fell 24% in the third. The latest trial flipped to -29% at -1.9 sd. A run of two early wins made 1.1 look settled, and it is not.", "when": ["11/09 07:33", "11/10 16:04", "11/11 22:09", "11/12 22:18", "11/15 21:53"]},\n{"rule": "CAP 0.75 -> 0.7 leans positive lately but is unsettled: +2%, +0%, -11%, +28% and +14% points/h (the last two at +1.0 sd and +0.7 sd). 0.75 -> 0.8 read -5%. Step it only as a new measurement at the value in force, judged on points/h, and take the point read, not the saved/deal gain.", "evidence": ["F53", "F56", "F67", "F80", "F85", "F93"], "status": "verified", "trap": "0.7 usually shows better saved/deal and a lower close rate (11/10 12:26, 11/15 11:23, 11/16 17:01), which makes it look like a safe win. 11/14 04:38 read -11% on points/h with a saved difference of -0.0 sd, and 11/12 05:22 read +0%. The two big gains came after the early flat reads, so the sign is not stable.", "when": ["11/10 03:43", "11/10 12:26", "11/12 05:22", "11/14 04:38", "11/15 11:23", "11/16 17:01"]},\n{"rule": "Do not add more break-rate, grade or over-cap penalties, or re-rankings by expected saved rate, to the buyer\'s candidate or scout ordering. Every such A/B lost: sort by surplus/list -2%, break-rate weighting -6%, grade penalty -4%, soft-break weighting -2%, scout re-rank by expected saved rate -5%, over-cap exponent 8 -9% close rate. The one ranking change that won went the other way: lowering a penalty (short-shelf-life food penalty 0.3 -> 0.05, +3% close rate).", "evidence": ["F37", "F39", "F42", "F55", "F59", "F66", "F58"], "status": "verified", "trap": "Each loser was written with an expected +1% to +4% and measured negative. 11/08 11:55, 11/08 16:36 and 11/10 09:28 were still labelled kept because they were judged on a part metric (buyer_saved_deal), which cost time before F41/F42/F59/F66 reverted the family. A plausible-sounding extra penal